{
  "description": "Issues #947, #879 and #980. `spec/error-model.md:165-169` is a delimiter-agnostic MUST NOT: \"on any recovery path, for any malformed input, a parser MUST NOT raise any unmatched-delimiter diagnostic -- the class whose members in v0.1 are `ol-unmatched-paren`, `ol-unmatched-brace`, and `ol-unmatched-bracket` -- for a delimiter that is, in fact, correctly matched in the source.\" Every program here is malformed and every delimiter in it is matched, so no member of that class may appear; `ol-bad-token` names the real defect instead. Measured at `ca653709` before the fix: each of the seven reserved-word rows raised `ol-unmatched-paren` TWICE on balanced parens and some named the offending word nowhere at all (#879), `print [ 1 + ]` raised `ol-unmatched-bracket`, and `print :nums[]` raised it against a `]` one character from its own `[`. This is mechanism B of the slice and it is NOT newline-related: every row is a single line, which is the measurement that separated #879's root from #980's -- they share the emission site, not the cause. It is also ONE recovery path reachable from several productions: parenthesized groups, list literals, dict literals, selectors and comprehension binders all route their \"expected a closer, found something else\" recovery through the same two places, which is why the fix is expressed once rather than per construct. Its partner `heritage/check/a-genuinely-unmatched-delimiter-is-still-reported` pins the opposite direction, without which a parser could satisfy this file by never emitting the class at all. A reserved word cannot be a `callable-name` (`spec/grammar.md:390`), which is what makes each paren row malformed.",
